WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.000
Now, let's chat about views, routes, and templates.

00:05.000 --> 00:14.000
Views and routes allow us to map URLs in our web application to pages, API, and points, and more.

00:14.000 --> 00:17.000
Our blog right now has three different sections.

00:17.000 --> 00:23.000
There's an index here that will show all of our posts.

00:23.000 --> 00:27.000
If I click on Read More, I go to a detail page.

00:27.000 --> 00:32.000
And I also have an about page right here that is at slash about.

00:32.000 --> 00:41.000
So, we have routes that are defined in at the project level in practical blog URLs.py and at the

00:41.000 --> 00:45.000
at level at blogslash URL.py.

00:45.000 --> 00:50.000
Let's take a quick look at those side by side.

00:50.000 --> 00:54.000
So, on the left hand side is the practical blog URL configuration.

00:54.000 --> 00:59.000
This is the top level configuration for everything.

00:59.000 --> 01:05.000
Here, I'm including a path to the admin, which we'll look at in just a second.

01:05.000 --> 01:10.000
It's one of the built-in things about Django that is really fantastic.

01:10.000 --> 01:14.000
The next thing is an include of blog.URLs.

01:14.000 --> 01:22.000
So, that's going to take all of the URLs that I've defined in my app specific blog.URLs and import them here.

01:22.000 --> 01:29.000
So, looking at URLs.py in blog, this is where you'll see I've defined the index,

01:29.000 --> 01:33.000
the about page and the detail page.

01:33.000 --> 01:41.000
And that is the path that I'm passing in as the first argument.

01:41.000 --> 01:45.000
We also have some templates.

01:45.000 --> 01:55.000
And those are located in the blog templates blog directory.

01:55.000 --> 01:59.000
So, a bit of a weird data structure, but that is what Django expects.

01:59.000 --> 02:04.000
We also have static blog CSS.

02:04.000 --> 02:13.000
If I take a look at base.html, this is just the base template that all of my other templates will use.

02:13.000 --> 02:18.000
It's got a header and a footer and all of those reusable elements.

02:18.000 --> 02:26.000
I need to call load static at the top here, which means load up all of the static resources I might need.

02:26.000 --> 02:29.000
Such as my CSS.

02:29.000 --> 02:36.000
These funky looking tags with the curly braces and the percent symbols are template tags.

02:36.000 --> 02:43.000
And those allow you to use some special commands in your HTML templates that let them interface with Django.

02:43.000 --> 02:47.000
Now, the important thing in base.html is this block right here.

02:47.000 --> 02:54.000
This content blocks, let's us know that we can use base.html as a template.

02:54.000 --> 03:00.000
If I then take a look at about.html, I have a template directive here that says,

03:00.000 --> 03:04.000
extend this one from blog base.html.

03:04.000 --> 03:22.000
And then everything that is in between these block content blocks insert that right into here, right into the matching content blocks.

03:22.000 --> 03:31.000
So that is pretty straightforward. We have a post list that has a little bit of a more complex template tag usage.

03:31.000 --> 03:33.000
Here we have a four loop.

03:33.000 --> 03:40.000
We are getting a post list variable passed into this template from our view, which we'll look at in just a second.

03:40.000 --> 03:45.000
And we're looping over it, so for every post that we have we're going to display it.

03:45.000 --> 03:57.000
And then for the detail, we are using a post variable and we're pulling out the post title, the post created at date and the post dot body.

03:57.000 --> 04:03.000
The slug is being used here for the read more button.

04:03.000 --> 04:14.000
We're passing in, we're using this URL template tag and we're passing in post, which is the name of the view.

04:14.000 --> 04:17.000
Followed by the slug.

04:17.000 --> 04:23.000
This matches our URL defined here in URLs.py.

04:23.000 --> 04:30.000
So that lets us get a link to the detail view.

04:30.000 --> 04:34.000
Now, we're going to take a quick look at views.

04:34.000 --> 04:40.000
These are some simple basic views that you might define when you're first starting out.

04:40.000 --> 04:45.000
But the more advanced concept is class-based views.

04:45.000 --> 04:50.000
So here we have our defined views.

04:50.000 --> 04:59.000
And these views map to the URLs that we have defined in your blog URLs.py.

04:59.000 --> 05:07.000
So here we see views.about views.index views.post.

05:07.000 --> 05:13.000
And we've also given these names so that we can access them from our templates.

05:13.000 --> 05:23.000
Now, this view, the about view, is just getting a template and rendering it and returning an HTTP response.

05:23.000 --> 05:34.000
This index view is taking a post, getting all of the objects in the database via query, creating a context.

05:34.000 --> 05:39.000
So we remember in our template earlier, we used a variable called post underscore list.

05:39.000 --> 05:46.000
This is where it came from, we're passing in here, and then for a detail view in our post.

05:46.000 --> 05:58.000
We're using a shortcut called get object or 404, which means that if a post with the passed in slot isn't found, Django will return a 404 status code.

05:58.000 --> 06:02.000
Now, this is a pattern that you will see over and over in Django.

06:02.000 --> 06:12.000
And so Django has built in some handy shortcuts called class-based views that allow you to take out a lot of this stuff that you might do over and over again.

06:12.000 --> 06:20.000
So I'm going to check out the class-based views branch in the repository.

06:20.000 --> 06:26.000
And we're going to take a look at what class-based views look like.

06:26.000 --> 06:28.000
This is it.

06:28.000 --> 06:41.000
So from Django.views.generic, I have imported detail view, which means give me one object, and I've imported list view, which gets a list of objects.

06:41.000 --> 06:46.000
And I've also imported the model that we care about in this case.

06:46.000 --> 06:47.000
It's post.

06:47.000 --> 06:54.000
So I'm creating a class-based view called post list view that inherits from list view, and I'm telling it what model to use.

06:54.000 --> 07:00.000
If we take a look at our URLs again, they look a little bit different.

07:00.000 --> 07:06.000
Now, you notice that I didn't have the about page in here.

07:06.000 --> 07:13.000
That's because I'm using a class-based view called detail view, our template view, and I'm using it right in URLs.

07:13.000 --> 07:20.000
So I'm calling a method on it called as view, and I'm telling it what template name I care about.

07:20.000 --> 07:22.000
Super quick shortcut.

07:22.000 --> 07:29.000
I'm also calling as view on my imported post list view and post detail view and giving it a name.

07:29.000 --> 07:36.000
Now, this code does the same exact thing that the other code was doing with a lot less craft.

07:36.000 --> 07:42.000
So let me make sure that my server is still running and take a look here, and here we are.

07:42.000 --> 07:45.000
Everything still works as expected.

